---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";

import articleHero from "../../../../assets/beer-marking-affordable-hero.png";
import LandingFooter from "../../../../components/LandingFooter.astro";
import LandingHeader from "../../../../components/LandingHeader.astro";
import { BEER_MARKING_COST_ARTICLE_EN } from "../../../../content/articles";
import { buildArticlePageGraph } from "../../../../lib/seo";
import { readPublicSiteConfig } from "../../../../lib/site-config";
import BaseLayout from "../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const article = BEER_MARKING_COST_ARTICLE_EN;
const config = readPublicSiteConfig({ PUBLIC_PHONE: import.meta.env.PUBLIC_PHONE });
---

<BaseLayout metadata={article} graph={buildArticlePageGraph(article)}>
  <LandingHeader page={article} phone={config.phone} />
  <main id="main" class="article-page">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="container article-hero__inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/en/">Markiro</a></li><li aria-current="page">{article.navigationLabel}</li></ol></nav>
        <div class="article-hero__layout">
          <div class="article-hero__copy">
            <p class="section-index">{article.eyebrow}</p>
            <h1>{article.heading}</h1>
            <p class="article-hero__lead">{article.introduction}</p>
            <div class="article-byline" aria-label="Publication details"><span>{article.authorName}</span><time datetime={article.publishedAt}>26 August 2026</time><span>{article.readingTimeMinutes} min read</span></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="article-hero__media" data-article-visual>
            <Picture src={articleHero} alt="Compact beer marking workstation beside a small packaging line" widths={[480, 720, 1080]} formats={["avif", "webp"]} sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 45vw" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" data-article-hero-image />
            <figcaption>A modern control loop can begin with one workstation rather than a plant-wide rebuild.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="container article-body">
      <aside class="article-summary" aria-labelledby="article-summary-title">
        <p class="section-index">Key takeaways</p><h2 id="article-summary-title">How to keep the launch budget controlled</h2>
        <ul>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Separate codes, consumables, equipment, software, implementation, and support in every proposal.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Do not replace a working computer, printer, or 2D scanner before a compatibility test on actual codes.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>For a smaller volume, manual application and one workstation can be a valid modern starting point.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Machine vision, automatic application, and rejection should solve measured throughput or quality constraints.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Compare total cost of ownership: downtime and manual reconciliation can outweigh a small licence-price difference.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Scale only after the process passes a pilot with actual packaging, speed, and recovery scenarios.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Article contents"><p class="section-index">Contents</p><ol><li><a href="#answer">Short answer</a></li><li><a href="#budget">Budget components</a></li><li><a href="#reuse">Reuse existing equipment</a></li><li><a href="#minimum">Minimum reliable loop</a></li><li><a href="#automation">Automation threshold</a></li><li><a href="#tco">Compare proposals</a></li><li><a href="#stages">Staged launch</a></li><li><a href="#markiro">Markiro accessibility</a></li></ol></nav>

      <section id="answer" aria-labelledby="answer-title">
        <p class="section-index">01 / Answer</p><h2 id="answer-title">What does beer marking cost in Russia for a smaller producer?</h2>
        <p><strong>There is no single project price, but there is also no mandatory high-end equipment package.</strong> Cost depends on application method, actual line speed, equipment already on site, aggregation scope, and number of lines. A lower-volume producer can start with one software workstation, a compatible printer, and a 2D scanner without immediately purchasing machine vision or an automatic applicator.</p>
        <p>Official Chestny ZNAK guidance says its technology-partner list is informational and participants may select <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/kakoe-oborudovanie-i-po-potrebuetsya-uchastniku-dlya-raboty-v-sisteme-markirovki-pivo">equipment at their own discretion</a>. For an international vendor or Russian manufacturer, that means compliance should be proven by function and acceptance testing rather than by buying one named bundle.</p>
        <div class="article-callout"><strong>Accessibility is not a compromise on control.</strong><p>Savings come from postponing automation that does not yet constrain production. Duplicate detection, GTIN validation, an operation journal, and restart recovery still belong in the first workstation.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="budget" aria-labelledby="budget-title">
        <p class="section-index">02 / Cost structure</p><h2 id="budget-title">Which components make up the beer marking budget?</h2>
        <p><strong>Separate mandatory variable costs, the initial working loop, and optional automation.</strong> This allows suppliers to be compared on the same basis and keeps a one-off purchase distinct from annual total cost.</p>
        <figure class="article-visual" data-article-visual>
          <img src="/images/articles/beer-marking-cost-budget-en.svg" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Three layers of a beer marking budget: mandatory costs, working loop, and later automation" data-article-diagram />
          <figcaption>Prove the working loop first. Add automation only to a process that already behaves correctly.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="article-cases">
          <article><h3>Codes and consumables</h3><p>Marking codes, label or another carrier, ribbon or ink, print-head maintenance, and control samples.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Equipment</h3><p>Computer or industrial terminal, printer, 2D scanner, network, and backup power—only where existing equipment fails acceptance.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Software and implementation</h3><p>Licence, job and label setup, hardware connection, training, pilot, support, and agreed integrations.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Automation</h3><p>Applicator, fixed vision, reject mechanism, sensors, guarding, and conveyor work form a separate engineering project.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p>As of 25 February 2026, the official source states that generation of one Russian beer marking code costs <strong>RUB 0.50 before VAT, or RUB 0.61 including 22% VAT</strong>. It also explains payment on issuance versus payment on application: <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/stoimost-i-oplata-kodov-markirovki-dlya-piva-i-pivnykh-napitkov">official code cost and payment methods</a>. All other budget components are facility-specific.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="reuse" aria-labelledby="reuse-title">
        <p class="section-index">03 / Inventory</p><h2 id="reuse-title">Which existing devices can remain in the project?</h2>
        <p><strong>Begin with an inventory, not a shopping cart.</strong> A compatible computer, label printer, and 2D area imager can often enter the pilot. A model name or connector is not enough, however: the printer must reproduce the required Data Matrix consistently, and the scanner must deliver the complete GS1 payload including control characters.</p>
        <ol class="article-steps">
          <li><h3>Check the workstation</h3><p>It should operate reliably in the production environment, have an explicit backup-power plan, and not depend on a shared office computer.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Run a real print test</h3><p>Use the intended substrate, label size, and speed. Test a series including the first labels after a controlled stop.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Capture the scanner stream</h3><p>Confirm GS1 Data Matrix decoding, GS separator preservation, and message termination in USB HID or serial mode.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Record the accepted configuration</h3><p>Keep the model, interface, parameters, and control sample. Only then is a device compatible with this facility’s line.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>Chestny ZNAK’s official community material for smaller beer producers discusses manual scanners, label printers, standard accounting software, and web services. It supports the principle that <a href="https://markirovka.ru/community/beer_producers/chto-neobkhodimo-dlya-tekhnicheskogo-osnashcheniya-proizvoditelyam-piva-pri-rabote-s-markirovkoy">the first production loop can remain compact</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="minimum" aria-labelledby="minimum-title">
        <p class="section-index">04 / First launch</p><h2 id="minimum-title">What belongs in a minimum reliable production loop?</h2>
        <p><strong>A minimum loop is not a spreadsheet workaround.</strong> It is a controlled workstation where an operator receives the production job, prints or uses prepared codes, scans items, sees a specific rejection reason, forms the case, prints the SSCC label, and leaves a recoverable local record.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>one dedicated workstation with local storage for the permitted production flow;</li>
          <li>a compatible ZPL or TSPL printer connected through Windows, TCP, or serial transport;</li>
          <li>a 2D scanner in keyboard-wedge or serial mode;</li>
          <li>format, GTIN, and duplicate checks before an item enters a case;</li>
          <li>controlled reprint and restart recovery;</li>
          <li>an outbound queue that prevents a network interruption from stopping packing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This architecture is not suitable for every throughput. For a smaller batch, however, it can provide modern traceability without rebuilding the conveyor. The pilot establishes the real boundary: sustained operator rate, queue formation, and failure conditions that justify mechanisation.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="automation" aria-labelledby="automation-title">
        <p class="section-index">05 / Next level</p><h2 id="automation-title">When does higher-cost automation become justified?</h2>
        <p><strong>Automate a measured constraint.</strong> If manual application maintains speed and print quality, the automatic applicator can wait. If the operator cannot keep pace, codes are missed, or the line stops for aiming, fixed vision and positive rejection become economically meaningful.</p>
        <p>Use pilot evidence: actual speed, repeated operations, downtime minutes, manual-reconciliation hours, scrap, and changeover time. Compare these losses with the acquisition and service cost of automation. Do not insert a device’s catalogue throughput into the business case without testing the actual package.</p>
        <p>Evaluate higher aggregation levels separately. Markiro’s current chain is item → case → SSCC. Pallet aggregation is the next development stage and should not inflate the initial budget where case-level operation is sufficient.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="tco" aria-labelledby="tco-title">
        <p class="section-index">06 / Comparison</p><h2 id="tco-title">Why a low bundle price may not mean a low total cost</h2>
        <p><strong>Compare total cost for the same period and volume, not only the first invoice.</strong> A low-cost printer with expensive media, proprietary label format, paid service for every changeover, or no recoverable journal may consume the initial saving quickly.</p>
        <div class="article-checklist">
          <div><h3>One-off</h3><ul><li>equipment and delivery;</li><li>configuration and integration;</li><li>label templates;</li><li>pilot and training.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>Recurring</h3><ul><li>licence and support;</li><li>codes and consumables;</li><li>print-component replacement;</li><li>updates and service.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>Operational loss</h3><ul><li>downtime and reconciliation;</li><li>wasted labels;</li><li>repeated operations;</li><li>single-specialist dependency.</li></ul></div>
        </div>
        <p>A proposal should separate software, implementation, equipment, consumables, integrations, and optional upgrades. That keeps an accessible project understandable after go-live, not just during the sale.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="stages" aria-labelledby="stages-title">
        <p class="section-index">07 / Scale-up</p><h2 id="stages-title">How can a staged launch avoid paying twice?</h2>
        <p><strong>Start with one line and representative SKU, but design the data so the pilot remains useful.</strong> The job, journal, case composition, and operation identifiers must retain their meaning when a second workstation is added.</p>
        <figure class="article-visual" data-article-visual>
          <img src="/images/articles/beer-marking-cost-stages-en.svg" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Three stages of an accessible launch: inventory, one-station pilot, and evidence-led scale-up" data-article-diagram />
          <figcaption>Each new expense appears only after the previous stage produces measurable evidence.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>First, accept or reject existing equipment. Second, run an actual production batch including network loss, a duplicate, and workstation restart. Third, add stations, vision, or an applicator only where the pilot journal exposes a constraint. This prevents an expensive architecture from being built around an inaccurately described process.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="markiro" aria-labelledby="markiro-title">
        <p class="section-index">08 / Markiro</p><h2 id="markiro-title">How Markiro keeps the solution accessible</h2>
        <p><strong>Markiro was conceived to give smaller producers a modern, simple, controlled production process without an excessive entry cost.</strong> The first loop can therefore be built around one workstation and compatible devices instead of a mandatory proprietary hardware cabinet. The configuration is proven on the actual line before scale-up.</p>
        <p>Markiro connects the job, scanner, printer, duplicate control, case composition, SSCC, and local journal. This software foundation remains useful when the next workstation is added. Mechanics, machine vision, and external integrations stay visible as separate options rather than hidden prerequisites.</p>
        <div class="article-callout article-callout--boundary"><strong>Accessibility does not mean one universal fixed price.</strong><p>The proposal depends on line count, genuinely missing equipment, application method, and integrations. Before pricing, we separate the required loop from options and test what the facility can already use.</p></div>
        <p>The current primary category is beer, beer-based beverages, and selected low-alcohol beverages. Additional product types are being introduced gradually.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
        <p class="section-index">Conclusion</p><h2 id="conclusion-title">A controlled budget needs precise architecture, not an outdated process</h2>
        <p>The most accessible launch does not remove controls; it avoids unnecessary automation. Test existing devices, build one reliable workstation, measure a production run, and scale the proven process.</p>
        <a class="button" href="/en/#demo" data-analytics="landing_demo_click" data-placement="article-conclusion">Scope the minimum production loop <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>

      <section class="article-related" aria-labelledby="article-related-title">
        <p class="section-index">Related</p><h2 id="article-related-title">Continue planning the line</h2>
        <div class="seo-related__grid"><a href="/en/articles/beer-marking-line-equipment/"><span>Beer marking line equipment</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/en/articles/beer-data-matrix-not-scanning/"><span>Beer Data Matrix diagnostics</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/en/packing-workstation/"><span>Packing workstation</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <LandingFooter page={article} />
  <script>import { browserLandingRuntime, initLanding } from "../../../../scripts/site"; initLanding(document, browserLandingRuntime(window));</script>
</BaseLayout>
